<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/12th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of al-Buhayra</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1130">1130</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of al-Buhayra was fought in 1130 near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh">Marrakesh</a> between the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> movement and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a>. The Almohads suffered a catastrophic defeat. The movement's founder <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-tumart">Ibn Tumart</a> died around this time, and leadership passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-mumin">Abd al-Mu'min</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In 1121 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-tumart">Ibn Tumart</a>, the founder and mahdi of the reformist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> movement, came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh">Marrakesh</a> to preach. He met the Almoravid emir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-ibn-yusuf">Ali ibn Yusuf</a> at prayers in the mosque and confronted him about his ways. The Almoravid leadership came to see his presence as subversive, and he fled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlas-mountains">Atlas Mountains</a>, where he established himself at Tinmal, south of the city. His power grew with the backing of allied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> tribes, above all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masmuda">Masmuda</a>, until he felt strong enough to attack the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a>. Ali ibn Yusuf, perhaps sensing the threat, gave Marrakesh its first ramparts in 1126.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The battle was the climax of an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> attempt on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh">Marrakesh</a>, the Almoravid capital. The Almohads first defeated an Almoravid force near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aghmat">Aghmat</a> and drove it back to the city. Their arrival caught the population by surprise: the defenders were routed, the inhabitants fled inside the walls, and some people died in the crush at the narrow gates.</p><p>Under the general al-Bashir and his lieutenant Abu Zakariyya, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohads</a> besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh">Marrakesh</a> for forty days. Then Almoravid reinforcements arrived and, together with a sortie by the defenders, crushed the besiegers in May 1130. The fighting took place just east of the city and is named after a garden, Buhayrat al-Raka'ik, that lay near the gates of Bab Debbagh and Bab Aylan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> commanders were killed. Al-Bashir died in the battle and Abu Zakariyya lost an eye to an arrow. The defeat kept the Almohads away from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh">Marrakesh</a> for 17 years. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-tumart">Ibn Tumart</a> died a few months later, and his death was kept secret for three years, a period known as the ghayba or occultation. When it was finally announced, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-mumin">Abd al-Mu'min</a> emerged as leader and founded a new dynasty, and in 1147 he conquered Marrakesh.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of al-Buhayra” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In its early years the movement fought the Almoravids by raids and skirmishes rather than conventional warfare, and in 1130 an Almohad attack on Marrakesh ended in a heavy defeat at the <b>Battle of al-Buhayra</b>; Ibn Tumart died a few months later.</blockquote><a href="/empire/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
